{=============================================================================
  Film condensation: heat-transfer coefficients
  CoolSolve Library model CSL-0089 (function library, kind = function)
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Six correlations for the heat-transfer coefficient h of condensing film,
  from the Nusselt analysis of a laminar film on a flat plate (1916) to the
  Shah (1979) correlation for condensation inside tubes:

    - h_Nusselt_laminar          laminar film on a (possibly inclined) plate
    - h_Boyko_Kruzhilin          laminar film in a vertical tube or bundle
    - h_Akers_Deans_Crosser      condensation in horizontal tubes
    - h_kinetic                  kinetic (diffusion) resistance of the vapor
    - h_Cavallini_Smith_Zecchin  forced-convection condensation in a tube
    - h_Shah                     condensation in a tube, reduced pressure form

  h_kinetic is the only one that is not a HTC of the film: it is the
  molecular-kinetic resistance of the vapor diffusing to the condensing
  surface, used as a correction of the condensation HTC.

  Five of them return the film heat-transfer coefficient h [W/m^2/K]; the two
  in-tube correlations (Boyko-Kruzhilin, Akers-Deans-Crosser) return the
  coefficient at the quality x of the interval. No property function is called
  inside the functions: the calling model passes the liquid and vapor
  properties, the mass flow rate, the geometry and (for the in-tube forms)
  the quality x.

  The demonstration program at the end calls every function: case A with the
  input set of the ht doctest of each correlation (published values), case B
  with one uniform, realistic input set (R134a condensing at 40 C in a 24 mm
  tube). Both cases are tabulated against ht in the README (verification
  table).

  Two conventions of the translation: the angle of h_Nusselt_laminar is in
  degrees, as the argument of ht, and the trigonometric functions of EES
  (setting DEG) and of CoolSolve take degrees (CoolSolve CS-DOC-TRIG), so
  sin(angle) is written where ht uses sin(angle*pi/180); the constant pi is
  written as a local pi_val in the function bodies, because CoolSolve does not
  resolve the pi constant inside a FUNCTION body (CoolSolve CS-BUG-PI-FUNCTION,
  registered with this card: it evaluates as 1 there).

  Inputs : Tsat, Tw [K], rhog, rhol [kg/m^3], kl [W/m-K], mul, mug [Pa-s],
           Cpl [J/kg-K], Hvap [J/kg], L [m], angle [deg], m [kg/s], D [m],
           x [-], T [K], P [Pa], MW [g/mol], f [-]
  Outputs: h [W/m^2/K] (one FUNCTION per correlation)
  Units  : SI - K, Pa, kg/s, kg/m^3, W/m/K, Pa-s, J/kg, J/kg/K, m, degrees
  Source : ht (Caleb Bell), ht/condensation.py, version 1.2.0, commit 85e0ee6,
           MIT - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht ; correlations by the authors
           cited in each function block - see README.md
  Status : VERIFIED against the doctests and the values of ht 1.2.0
           (README.md, "Verification")
=============================================================================}

FUNCTION h_Nusselt_laminar(Tsat, Tw, rhog, rhol, kl, mul, Hvap, L, angle)
{H_NUSSELT_LAMINAR - heat-transfer coefficient of the laminar film of a pure
chemical condensing on a flat plate, from the Nusselt analysis of 1916; the
plate may be inclined.

  h = 0.943*[g*sin(theta)*rhol*(rhol - rhog)*kl^3*Hvap
             /(mul*(Tsat - Tw)*L)]^0.25

  the constant 0.943 is 2*sqrt(2)/3, as in the original

Tsat [K] saturation temperature at the operating pressure; Tw [K] wall
temperature; rhog [kg/m^3] density of the gas; rhol [kg/m^3] density of the
liquid; kl [W/m/K] thermal conductivity of the liquid; mul [Pa-s] viscosity of
the liquid; Hvap [J/kg] heat of vaporization at that pressure; L [m] length of
the plate; angle [deg] inclination of the plate (90 = vertical, as in ht).
Validity: none quoted in ht (laminar film, pure substance).
Original: Nusselt (1916), as presented in Hewitt, G. L., G. F. Shires and
  T. R. Bott, Process Heat Transfer, 1E, CRC Press, Boca Raton, 1994, p. 578.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/condensation.py, Nusselt_laminar,
  MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  g_acc = 9.80665  "standard acceleration of gravity [m/s^2]"
  "angle is in degrees, as in ht: the trigonometric functions of EES (setting DEG) and of CoolSolve take degrees"
  h_Nusselt_laminar = 2*2^0.5/3*(g_acc*sin(angle)*rhol*(rhol - rhog)*kl^3*Hvap/(mul*(Tsat - Tw)*L))^0.25
END

FUNCTION h_Boyko_Kruzhilin(m, rhog, rhol, kl, mul, Cpl, D, x)
{H_BOYKO_KRUZHILIN - heat-transfer coefficient of the film of a pure chemical
condensing inside a vertical tube or a tube bundle, at the quality x of the
interval, as presented in [2]_ according to [1]_.

  h_f   = h_LO*[1 + x*(rhol/rhog - 1)]^0.5
  h_LO  = 0.021*kl/D*Re_LO^0.8*Pr_l^0.43

m [kg/s] mass flow rate; rhog [kg/m^3] density of the gas; rhol [kg/m^3]
density of the liquid; kl [W/m/K] thermal conductivity of the liquid; mul
[Pa-s] viscosity of the liquid; Cpl [J/kg/K] constant-pressure heat capacity
of the liquid; D [m] diameter of the tubing; x [-] quality at the specific
interval. The mass flow rate is converted into the liquid-only superficial
velocity V_lo = m/rhol/(pi/4*D^2) and Re_lo = rhol*V_lo*D/mul.
Validity: none quoted in ht (vertical tube or bundle, pure substance).
Note: the docstring of ht writes the length L in h_LO; the code of ht, which
  reproduces the published value, uses the tube diameter D - as here.
Original: [1] Boyko, L. D. and G. N. Kruzhilin, "Heat Transfer and Hydraulic
  Resistance during Condensation of Steam in a Horizontal Tube and in a Bundle
  of Tubes", Int. J. Heat Mass Transfer 10(3): 361-373, 1 March 1967;
  [2] Hewitt, G. L., G. F. Shires and T. R. Bott, Process Heat Transfer, 1E,
  CRC Press, Boca Raton, 1994, p. 589.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/condensation.py, Boyko_Kruzhilin,
  MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  pi_val = 3.141592653589793  "pi, as a local constant (see the header)"
  V_lo = m/rhol/(pi_val/4*D^2)
  Re_lo = rhol*V_lo*D/mul
  Pr_l = mul*Cpl/kl
  h_lo = 0.021*kl/D*Re_lo^0.8*Pr_l^0.43
  h_Boyko_Kruzhilin = h_lo*(1 + x*(rhol/rhog - 1))^0.5
END

FUNCTION h_Akers_Deans_Crosser(m, rhog, rhol, kl, mul, Cpl, D, x)
{H_AKERS_DEANS_CROSSER - heat-transfer coefficient of a pure chemical
condensing inside a horizontal tube, at the quality x of the interval, as
presented in [2]_ according to [1]_.

  Nu  = h*Di/kl = C*Re_e^n*Pr_l^(1/3)
  C = 0.0265, n = 0.8   for Re_e > 5E4
  C = 5.03,   n = 1/3   for Re_e < 5E4
  Re_e = Di*Ge/mul
  Ge   = G*[(1 - x) + x*(rhol/rhog)^0.5]

m [kg/s] mass flow rate; rhog [kg/m^3] density of the gas; rhol [kg/m^3]
density of the liquid; kl [W/m/K] thermal conductivity of the liquid; mul
[Pa-s] viscosity of the liquid; Cpl [J/kg/K] constant-pressure heat capacity
of the liquid; D [m] diameter of the tubing (Di above); x [-] quality at the
specific interval.
Validity: the two branches of the original, Re_e > 5E4 and Re_e < 5E4 (as in
  ht); no other limit is quoted.
Original: [1] Akers, W. W., H. A. Deans and O. K. Crosser, "Condensing Heat
  Transfer Within Horizontal Tubes", Chem. Eng. Progr. Vol. 55, Symposium
  Ser. No. 29, 1 January 1959; [2] Kakaç, S. (ed.), Boilers, Evaporators, and
  Condensers, 1st ed., Wiley-Interscience, New York, 1991.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/condensation.py,
  Akers_Deans_Crosser, MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  pi_val = 3.141592653589793  "pi, as a local constant (see the header)"
  G = m/(pi_val/4*D^2)
  Ge = G*((1 - x) + x*(rhol/rhog)^0.5)
  Re_e = D*Ge/mul
  Pr_l = mul*Cpl/kl
  IF Re_e > 5E4 THEN
    C = 0.0265
    n = 0.8
  ELSE
    C = 5.03
    n = 1/3
  ENDIF
  Nu = C*Re_e^n*Pr_l^(1/3)
  h_Akers_Deans_Crosser = Nu*kl/D
END

FUNCTION h_kinetic(T, P, MW, Hvap, f)
{H_KINETIC - heat-transfer coefficient of the kinetic (diffusion) resistance
of the vapor on a condensing surface, as presented in [2]_ according to [1]_.

  h = (2f/(2 - f))*(MW/(1000*2*pi*R_u*T))^0.5*(Hvap^2*P*MW)/(1000*R_u*T^2)

T [K] vapor temperature; P [Pa] vapor pressure; MW [g/mol] molecular weight of
the gas; Hvap [J/kg] heat of vaporization of the fluid at that pressure;
f [-] correction factor for how the removal of gas particles affects the
behaviour of the ideal gas diffusing to the condensing surface (it is quite
close to one and has not been well explored in the literature, as in the
original); ht gives it a default value of 1, here it is an argument.
Validity: none quoted in ht.
Original: [1] Berman, L. D., "On the Effect of Molecular-Kinetic Resistance
  upon Heat Transfer with Condensation", Int. J. Heat Mass Transfer 10(10):
  1463, 1 October 1967; [2] Kakaç, S. (ed.), Boilers, Evaporators, and
  Condensers, 1st ed., Wiley-Interscience, New York, 1991. Also in Stephan,
  K., Heat Transfer in Condensation and Boiling, Springer, Berlin, 1992/2013.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/condensation.py, h_kinetic,
  MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  pi_val = 3.141592653589793  "pi, as a local constant (see the header)"
  R_u = 8.31446261815324  "universal gas constant [J/(mol K)]"
  h_kinetic = 2*f/(2 - f)*(MW/(1000*2*pi_val*R_u*T))^0.5*(Hvap^2*P*MW)/(1000*R_u*T^2)
END

FUNCTION h_Cavallini_Smith_Zecchin(m, x, D, rhol, rhog, mul, mug, kl, Cpl)
{H_CAVALLINI_SMITH_ZECCHIN - heat-transfer coefficient of the film of a fluid
condensing inside a tube, as presented in [1]_, also given in [2]_ and [3]_.

  Nu    = h*Di/kl = 0.05*Re_eq^0.8*Pr_l^0.33
  Re_eq = Re_g*(mug/mul)*(rhol/rhog)^0.5 + Re_l
  v_gs  = m*x/(rhog*pi/4*D^2)
  v_ls  = m*(1 - x)/(rhol*pi/4*D^2)

m [kg/s] mass flow rate; x [-] quality at the specific interval; D [m] diameter
of the channel; rhol [kg/m^3] and rhog [kg/m^3] densities of the liquid and of
the gas; mul [Pa-s] and mug [Pa-s] viscosities of the liquid and of the gas;
kl [W/m/K] thermal conductivity of the liquid; Cpl [J/kg/K] constant-pressure
heat capacity of the liquid. The Nusselt number is based on the liquid
properties and on the tube diameter (as in the original).
Validity: none quoted in ht (forced-convection condensation in a tube).
Original: [1] A. Cavallini, J. R. Smith and R. Zecchin, "A Dimensionless
  Correlation for Heat Transfer in Forced Convection Condensation", 6th
  International Heat Transfer Conference, Tokyo, Japan, 1974, pp. 309-313;
  [2] Kakaç, S. (ed.), Boilers, Evaporators, and Condensers, 1st ed.,
  Wiley-Interscience, New York, 1991; [3] Balcilar, M., A. S. Dalkiliç,
  B. Bolat and S. Wongwises, "Investigation of Empirical Correlations on the
  Determination of Condensation Heat Transfer Characteristics during Downward
  Annular Flow of R134a inside a Vertical Smooth Tube Using Artificial
  Intelligence Algorithms", J. Mech. Sci. Technol. 25(10): 2683-2701,
  12 October 2011.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/condensation.py,
  Cavallini_Smith_Zecchin, MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  Pr_l = mul*Cpl/kl
  pi_val = 3.141592653589793  "pi, as a local constant (see the header)"
  V_l = m*(1 - x)/(rhol*pi_val/4*D^2)
  V_g = m*x/(rhog*pi_val/4*D^2)
  Re_l = rhol*V_l*D/mul
  Re_g = rhog*V_g*D/mug
  Re_eq = Re_g*(mug/mul)*(rhol/rhog)^0.5 + Re_l
  Nu_l = 0.05*Re_eq^0.8*Pr_l^0.33
  h_Cavallini_Smith_Zecchin = Nu_l*kl/D
END

FUNCTION h_Shah(m, x, D, rhol, mul, kl, Cpl, P, Pc)
{H_SHAH - heat-transfer coefficient of the film of a fluid condensing inside a
tube, as presented in [1]_ and again by the same author in [2]_, also given in
[3]_. No property of the gas is required.

  h_TP = h_L*[(1 - x)^0.8 + 3.8*x^0.76*(1 - x)^0.04/Pr^0.38]

The single-phase coefficient h_L is the Dittus-Boelter correlation, with a
Reynolds number assuming all the flow is liquid (ht calls
turbulent_Dittus_Boelter with its default arguments, heating = 1 and
revised = 1, i.e. Nu = 0.023*Re^0.8*Pr^0.4; the correlation is also
CSL-0087, function Nu_Dittus_Boelter).

m [kg/s] mass flow rate; x [-] quality at the specific interval; D [m] diameter
of the channel; rhol [kg/m^3] density of the liquid; mul [Pa-s] viscosity of
the liquid; kl [W/m/K] thermal conductivity of the liquid; Cpl [J/kg/K]
constant-pressure heat capacity of the liquid; P [Pa] pressure of the fluid;
Pc [Pa] critical pressure of the fluid.
Validity: none quoted in ht (condensation inside a tube).
Original: [1] Shah, M. M., "A General Correlation for Heat Transfer during
  Film Condensation inside Pipes", Int. J. Heat Mass Transfer 22(4): 547-556,
  1 April 1979; [2] Shah, M. M., "Heat Transfer during Film Condensation in
  Tubes and Annuli: A Review of the Literature", ASHRAE Transactions 87(3):
  1086-1100, 1981; [3] Kakaç, S. (ed.), Boilers, Evaporators, and Condensers,
  1st ed., Wiley-Interscience, New York, 1991.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/condensation.py, Shah,
  MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  pi_val = 3.141592653589793  "pi, as a local constant (see the header)"
  V_L = m/(rhol*pi_val/4*D^2)
  Re_L = rhol*V_L*D/mul
  Pr_l = mul*Cpl/kl
  Nu_L = 0.023*Re_L^0.8*Pr_l^0.4  "Dittus-Boelter, heating and revised coefficients, as in ht"
  h_L = Nu_L*kl/D
  Pr_reduced = P/Pc
  h_Shah = h_L*((1 - x)^0.8 + 3.8*x^0.76*(1 - x)^0.04/Pr_reduced^0.38)
END

"!Demonstration program: every correlation is called twice"
"It is ignored by the future $INCLUDE library:condensation_film (CS-FEAT-IMPORT)."
"Case A: the input set of the ht doctest of each correlation (published values)."
"Case B: one uniform, realistic input set - R134a condensing at 40 C in a 24 mm"
"tube, 0.4 kg/s, quality 0.6, wall 10 K below the saturation temperature."
"Both cases are tabulated against ht in the README (verification table)."

"Case A - input sets of the ht doctests"
Tsat_A = 370
Tw_A = 350
rhog_A = 7.0
rhol_A = 585
kl_A = 0.091
mul_A = 158.9E-6
Hvap_A = 776900
L_A = 0.1
angle_A = 90
angle_incl_A = 45
"in-tube correlations of the doctests (steam)"
rhog_BK_A = 6.36
rhol_BK_A = 582.9
kl_BK_A = 0.098
mul_BK_A = 159E-6
Cpl_BK_A = 2520
D_BK_A = 0.03
x_BK_A = 0.85
m_Boyko_A = 500*pi/4*0.03^2
m_Akers_A = 0.35
m_Akers_low_A = 0.02
T_kin_A = 300
P_kin_A = 1E5
Hvap_kin_A = 2441674  "water at 1 bar and 300 K, as in the ht doctest"
MW_A = 18.02
f_A = 0.9
m_Cav_A = 1
x_Cav_A = 0.4
D_Cav_A = 0.3
rhol_Cav_A = 800
rhog_Cav_A = 2.5
mul_Cav_A = 1E-5
mug_Cav_A = 1E-3
kl_Cav_A = 0.6
Cpl_Cav_A = 2300
m_Shah_A = 1
x_Shah_A = 0.4
D_Shah_A = 0.3
rhol_Shah_A = 800
mul_Shah_A = 1E-5
kl_Shah_A = 0.6
Cpl_Shah_A = 2300
P_Shah_A = 1E6
Pc_Shah_A = 2E7

"h_Nusselt_laminar - vertical plate, then the same plate inclined at 45 degrees"
h_Nusselt_laminar_A = h_Nusselt_laminar(Tsat_A, Tw_A, rhog_A, rhol_A, kl_A, mul_A, Hvap_A, L_A, angle_A)
h_Nusselt_laminar_inclined_A = h_Nusselt_laminar(Tsat_A, Tw_A, rhog_A, rhol_A, kl_A, mul_A, Hvap_A, L_A, angle_incl_A)
"h_Boyko_Kruzhilin - steam in a 30 mm tube at x = 0.85"
h_Boyko_Kruzhilin_A = h_Boyko_Kruzhilin(m_Boyko_A, rhog_BK_A, rhol_BK_A, kl_BK_A, mul_BK_A, Cpl_BK_A, D_BK_A, x_BK_A)
"h_Akers_Deans_Crosser - Re_e = 7.7E5 (high branch), then m = 0.02 kg/s, Re_e = 4.4E4 (low branch)"
h_Akers_Deans_Crosser_A = h_Akers_Deans_Crosser(m_Akers_A, rhog_BK_A, rhol_BK_A, kl_BK_A, mul_BK_A, Cpl_BK_A, D_BK_A, x_BK_A)
h_Akers_Deans_Crosser_lowRe_A = h_Akers_Deans_Crosser(m_Akers_low_A, rhog_BK_A, rhol_BK_A, kl_BK_A, mul_BK_A, Cpl_BK_A, D_BK_A, x_BK_A)
"h_kinetic - water at 1 bar and 300 K, f = 1 (as in the ht doctest), then f = 0.9"
h_kinetic_A = h_kinetic(T_kin_A, P_kin_A, MW_A, Hvap_kin_A, 1)
h_kinetic_f_A = h_kinetic(T_kin_A, P_kin_A, MW_A, Hvap_kin_A, f_A)
"h_Cavallini_Smith_Zecchin - the input set of the ht doctest"
h_Cavallini_Smith_Zecchin_A = h_Cavallini_Smith_Zecchin(m_Cav_A, x_Cav_A, D_Cav_A, rhol_Cav_A, rhog_Cav_A, mul_Cav_A, mug_Cav_A, kl_Cav_A, Cpl_Cav_A)
"h_Shah - the input set of the ht doctest"
h_Shah_A = h_Shah(m_Shah_A, x_Shah_A, D_Shah_A, rhol_Shah_A, mul_Shah_A, kl_Shah_A, Cpl_Shah_A, P_Shah_A, Pc_Shah_A)

"Case B - second, uniform input set: R134a condensing at 40 C in a 24 mm tube"
m_B = 0.4
Tsat_B = 313.15
Tw_B = 303.15
rhog_B = 30
rhol_B = 1100
kl_B = 0.13
mul_B = 2.5E-4
mug_B = 1.2E-5
Cpl_B = 1450
Hvap_B = 1.7E5
L_B = 0.5
angle_B = 90
angle_incl_B = 60
D_B = 0.024
x_B = 0.6
P_B = 1.6E6
Pc_B = 4.059E6
MW_B = 102.03
f_B = 0.95

"h_Nusselt_laminar - vertical plate, then the same plate inclined at 60 degrees"
h_Nusselt_laminar_B = h_Nusselt_laminar(Tsat_B, Tw_B, rhog_B, rhol_B, kl_B, mul_B, Hvap_B, L_B, angle_B)
h_Nusselt_laminar_inclined_B = h_Nusselt_laminar(Tsat_B, Tw_B, rhog_B, rhol_B, kl_B, mul_B, Hvap_B, L_B, angle_incl_B)
"h_Boyko_Kruzhilin - x = 0.6"
h_Boyko_Kruzhilin_B = h_Boyko_Kruzhilin(m_B, rhog_B, rhol_B, kl_B, mul_B, Cpl_B, D_B, x_B)
"h_Akers_Deans_Crosser - Re_e = 3.4E5 (high branch), then m = 0.02 kg/s, Re_e = 1.7E4 (low branch)"
h_Akers_Deans_Crosser_B = h_Akers_Deans_Crosser(m_B, rhog_B, rhol_B, kl_B, mul_B, Cpl_B, D_B, x_B)
h_Akers_Deans_Crosser_lowRe_B = h_Akers_Deans_Crosser(m_B/20, rhog_B, rhol_B, kl_B, mul_B, Cpl_B, D_B, x_B)
"h_kinetic - f = 1, then f = 0.95"
h_kinetic_B = h_kinetic(Tsat_B, P_B, MW_B, Hvap_B, 1)
h_kinetic_f_B = h_kinetic(Tsat_B, P_B, MW_B, Hvap_B, f_B)
"h_Cavallini_Smith_Zecchin - x = 0.6"
h_Cavallini_Smith_Zecchin_B = h_Cavallini_Smith_Zecchin(m_B, x_B, D_B, rhol_B, rhog_B, mul_B, mug_B, kl_B, Cpl_B)
"h_Shah - x = 0.6"
h_Shah_B = h_Shah(m_B, x_B, D_B, rhol_B, mul_B, kl_B, Cpl_B, P_B, Pc_B)
